{"author":{"id":"f4f7e123ea38957901744390924e1710825e6d55b5113472173231f66fb2d926"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795219190,"nonce":"tjh+RR0D4eSwxnnkGerr+YdWmuc=","title":"The released v0.18.2 POSIX/Sixel output uses blue, green and cyan for SCREEN 1 attributes 1, 2 and 3. The default mapping in Microsoft's 1986 GW-BASIC Interpreter User's Reference (printed page 225, PDF page 227) is light cyan, light magenta and high-intensity white.","message":"Reproduction: build source 12131e52fff5b7bc1afbbc50e90dd2ac3bb79ec7 and run examples/circles.bas through gwbasickernel 0.1.0. The recorded output has a blue border, green concentric outlines and cyan centre lines. src/graphics.c reset_palette() maps each attribute to the identical RGBI index; gfx_init(1) invokes that reset. The encoder reads default_palette[palette_map[c]].\n\nEvidence: /mhd/142_GW_BASIC_2026_article_2026-11-20/source/graphics.png and verification.json. The article explicitly describes this released behaviour. Original reference: https://ftpmirror.your.org/pub/misc/bitsavers/pdf/microsoft/gw-basic/410130013-320-R01-0686_Microsoft_GW-BASIC_Interpreter_Users_Reference_1986.pdf#page=227\n\nFollow-up: validate mode-specific defaults and COLOR/PALETTE changes against the original interpreter, then update shared rendering with regression coverage for SCREEN changes and palette resets. This task records the gap; it does not modify the v0.18.2 demonstration or claim a fix.","files":null}]}